      "instructions": "1-6: Complete the appointment form. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n7-10: Answer the questions. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Hana Rangi: Good morning, Harbour Document Centre. Hana speaking. We arrange witnessed signatures and certified copies by appointment. How can I help?\nTessa Whitlock: Hello. I need certified copies for an overseas application. I began the online booking, but I was not sure which service to choose or what identification to bring.\nHana Rangi: I can complete the booking with you. First, which original document needs to be copied? We handle certificates, qualifications and several other records, but the original must be available for comparison.\nTessa Whitlock: It is my birth certificate. I also have a university transcript, although the organisation has asked for an ordinary scan of that, so it does not need certification.\nHana Rangi: Fine. How many certified copies of the birth certificate are required? The booking page sometimes carries over the number from a previous enquiry.\nTessa Whitlock: I first selected two, then checked the application letter again. It asks for three copies, so please put three on the appointment form.\nHana Rangi: We have Tuesday afternoon at twenty past three, Wednesday morning at ten forty, or Friday just after midday. The Wednesday slot allows extra time if all three copies need separate marks.\nTessa Whitlock: Wednesday at ten forty would be best. Friday was my first thought, but I need to post the application before then.\nHana Rangi: For identity, bring a current photo document. A driving licence is accepted, but you mentioned in your form that its address is old. Your passport will be simpler because it is current.\nTessa Whitlock: Then I will bring my passport, along with the original certificate. I will leave the old driving licence at home so there is no confusion.\nHana Rangi: Would you like to collect the copies, or should we return them? Standard post is inexpensive, while our courier return includes tracking and requires a signature.\nTessa Whitlock: Please use the courier. I considered collecting them, but I will be outside Wellington for work the next day and tracking would be reassuring.\nHana Rangi: That is noted. A solicitor is available at that appointment. A Justice of the Peace also certifies copies in many situations, but none is rostered here on Wednesday morning.\nTessa Whitlock: A solicitor is acceptable for this application. Does each copy simply receive a signature, or is anything else required?\nHana Rangi: The certifier compares each copy with the original and adds the required statement. Beside the signature, the date must be written clearly. Please do not write on the copies yourself before the appointment.\nTessa Whitlock: Understood. Apart from the originals and my passport, should I bring proof of what the receiving organisation wants?\nHana Rangi: Yes, bring the application letter. It lets the solicitor check whether the receiving organisation has specified particular wording. A screenshot on your phone is not necessary if you have the letter.\nTessa Whitlock: I have the printed letter, so I will add it to the folder. How do I recognise the return parcel if a courier message arrives while I am travelling?\nHana Rangi: Your confirmation email will contain a reference number. The same reference number appears on the courier label and in the tracking message. It is more reliable than searching by your surname.\nTessa Whitlock: Perfect. So the booking is Wednesday at ten forty for three certified copies of my birth certificate, with courier return.\nHana Rangi: Correct. Bring the original birth certificate, your passport and the application letter. We will email the reference number within a few minutes. Please arrive five minutes early, but there is no need to come before the centre opens.",
