      "instructions": "11-15: Choose the correct instruction, A-G, for each item.\n16-20: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Maeve Donnelly: Welcome to the Lee Valley paddling centre. I am Maeve, and I will lead today’s beginner session. Before we enter the water, I will explain the equipment and the changing arrangements.\nMaeve Donnelly: Buoyancy aids are arranged by size near the door, but please do not pick one up immediately. I have the booking list and will call surnames in order. Wait until you hear yours before taking an aid; we need to record the size issued.\nMaeve Donnelly: Whistles arrive clipped to the aids, although they are logged separately. At the end, do not put yours into the general equipment crate. Unclip it and hand it back separately; reception staff count them before the next group.\nMaeve Donnelly: Each pair has a spare paddle. Before you carry it down the path, show it to the assistant at the riverside gate. She will check the blade number against your boat; the check is not done at the equipment rack.\nMaeve Donnelly: We also lend dry bags for small essentials. They remain behind the desk while I give this safety talk, so do not take one from the bench. When the talk ends, collect the bag from reception and write its number on your locker slip.\nMaeve Donnelly: The throw line is already secured beside the bank assistant's position. Leave it where it is when you move the boats; a trained staff member will remove it if needed. Beginners should not carry it to the water or practise with it today.\nMaeve Donnelly: Now, changing rooms. The upstairs room looks larger, but building work has closed it this week. Use the ground-floor changing room beside reception. The outdoor shelter is for wet equipment after the session, not for changing clothes.\nMaeve Donnelly: Once changed, place bags in the lockers and meet beside the equipment rack. We launch in a fixed order. The instructor enters first and waits downstream. Paddlers then launch one at a time. The bank assistant goes last after checking that nothing has been left behind.\nMaeve Donnelly: You may hear three possible signals. One short whistle means look towards the instructor and wait for a hand signal. Two short blasts mean gather near the bank. A continuous blast means leave the water at the nearest safe landing point. Today we will practise the two-blast signal before travelling upstream.\nMaeve Donnelly: People often ask whether light rain cancels a session. Usually it does not. We check river level, wind and official weather information together. If conditions become unsafe, the centre manager—not an individual participant—makes the cancellation decision and sends a text to the booking contact.\nMaeve Donnelly: If you receive no text, check in at reception. A warning triggers a fresh risk assessment. We may shorten the route or keep the group on the sheltered practice channel.\nMaeve Donnelly: At the water’s edge, keep the boat parallel to the bank while the assistant steadies it. Sit down before placing both feet inside. Do not push off until the instructor calls your name. That prevents two beginners from entering the narrow channel together.\nMaeve Donnelly: During the trip, stay between the lead instructor and the bank assistant. If you lose sight of the person ahead, stop in calm water rather than racing to catch up. Raise your paddle horizontally only if you need help; it is not our signal for an ordinary rest.\nMaeve Donnelly: When we return, carry the boats to the marked stands. Leave wet buoyancy aids on the ventilated rail, not in your locker. Rinse the paddles at the hose, but report any damage before you clean it, so staff can see the problem clearly.\nMaeve Donnelly: Finally, remember the order: change downstairs, fit and check equipment, meet at the rack, then launch one at a time behind the instructor. We will now divide into pairs and practise fitting the buoyancy aids before walking to the river.",
