      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct revision, A-G, for each programme item.\n26-30: Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Amira Voss: We need to repair the running order for tomorrow’s twenty-eight-minute training programme. Since Professor Lane cancelled, I have moved the recorded market interview into his slot, but the timings still do not close.\nEthan Sloane: Let us work in transmission order. The opening headlines currently take ninety seconds because each story has background detail. They should orient listeners, not repeat the later packages.\nAmira Voss: So for the opening headlines, we shorten the summary and keep only one sentence per story. I had considered dropping the local arts item entirely, but its full report appears later and still needs a headline.\nEthan Sloane: Next is the recorded market interview. The raw interview lasts four minutes forty. The strongest answer begins after a long introduction, so trim the insert to two minutes and start with the vendor explaining the price change.\nAmira Voss: Agreed. I will keep the natural breath before her answer, but remove the presenter’s repeated question. That should sound edited rather than rushed.\nEthan Sloane: The traffic bulletin is scheduled after the culture review, yet the road closure begins during our broadcast. Move that bulletin earlier, immediately after the opening headlines, so the information reaches commuters in time.\nAmira Voss: Earlier makes sense. Now the empty studio-guest slot: I suggested extending the culture review, but we do not have enough checked material for another three minutes.\nEthan Sloane: Use selected listener messages instead. We already have permission to broadcast five, and the presenter can read three contrasting responses. That replaces the cancelled guest without pretending we have a new interview.\nAmira Voss: For the closing trail, the recorded version advertises next week’s guest by name. Since the booking is not confirmed, we should replace the name with the programme topic rather than delete the whole trail.\nEthan Sloane: Yes, revise the wording so it promises a discussion about neighbourhood transport, not a particular speaker. That is our fifth rundown change.\nAmira Voss: I am updating my paper copy as we talk. Should I send you a photograph when it is finished?\nEthan Sloane: Better to put every change in the shared rundown. A photograph cannot show whether the presenter, studio manager and news desk are looking at the same version.\nAmira Voss: Right. We should backtime from the fixed news bulletin at half past the hour. Its start cannot move, so our programme must finish cleanly before that feed opens.\nEthan Sloane: Exactly. With the market insert limited to two minutes, the revised total becomes twenty-seven minutes forty. That leaves twenty seconds for the continuity cue and a safe handover.\nAmira Voss: The presenter needs to recognise that cue instantly. Last week we wrote it in black like the ordinary notes.\nEthan Sloane: This time put the continuity cue in red. Do not colour the whole final line; only the words that tell the presenter to stop and hand to news.\nAmira Voss: I will also mark the music fades. The opening bed ends under the first headline, and the closing bed begins only after the listener messages.\nEthan Sloane: Good. Before rehearsal, we need a final check of pronunciations. The street name in the traffic item and the artist’s surname in the culture review are both easy to misread.\nAmira Voss: I can confirm them with the contributors and add phonetic notes. What about the presenter links between items? Some still refer to Professor Lane.\nEthan Sloane: Rewrite those links after the order is locked. Otherwise you may polish wording that moves again. The listener-message introduction should explain that the guest cancelled, but it does not need to describe why.\nEthan Sloane: Rehearse the exact two-minute insert that will be transmitted. If the edit feels abrupt, we fix it now. Using a different rehearsal file creates false confidence about the timing.\nAmira Voss: Then I will export the final insert, label it clearly and link it from the shared rundown. I will remove the older draft from the playout list, though I will keep it in the project archive.\nEthan Sloane: I will move the traffic bulletin and prepare the three listener messages. Once your timings are entered, we can perform a backtime check from the news bulletin and then read every transition aloud.\nAmira Voss: So our final plan is: shortened headlines, traffic earlier, a two-minute market insert, listener messages in the guest slot, and a topic-only closing trail. All changes go in the shared rundown.\nEthan Sloane: And the continuity cue is red, with pronunciations checked before rehearsal. That should make the programme both accurate and calm under live conditions.",
