      "title": "Listening — Part 4: Academic monologue: urban nitrate aerosols at a Toronto university",
      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the flow chart. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n37-40: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Today we will examine nitrate in fine urban particles. Nitrate is not usually emitted as a finished particle in the way that road dust is. It forms through atmospheric reactions, so we need to follow the gases, the chemistry and the conditions that shift material between gas and particle phases.\nLecturer: The first precursor is nitrogen oxides, often written as NOx. In cities, important sources include vehicles and combustion. These gases undergo oxidation in the atmosphere. The chemistry differs between day and night, but both pathways can ultimately produce nitric acid.\nLecturer: The second important gas is ammonia. Agricultural activity is a major source over broad regions, although urban sources also exist. Air masses do not respect municipal boundaries, so ammonia formed outside a city can influence particulate nitrate measured within it.\nLecturer: Now consider the simplified sequence. Nitrogen oxides are oxidised to nitric acid. Nitric acid then reacts with ammonia. One important product is ammonium nitrate, which can enter the fine-particle fraction. This diagram is a teaching simplification: real air contains competing compounds and surfaces.\nLecturer: Whether ammonium nitrate remains in particles depends strongly on temperature. Low temperatures generally favour the particle phase, while warmer conditions encourage some material to return to gases. Relative humidity and the presence of other ions also matter, so temperature alone does not predict every observation. Later, laboratories analyse filter samples for those ions.\nLecturer: This helps explain why nitrate often shows a seasonal pattern. A cold winter atmosphere can support particulate nitrate, and a winter inversion may trap pollutants close to the ground. In summer, stronger mixing can dilute local concentrations and greater heat can shift volatile material away from particles.\nLecturer: Notice that the inversion is a meteorological condition, not a chemical ingredient. It limits vertical mixing. If precursor gases and newly formed particles remain in a shallow layer, concentrations near monitoring sites can rise even when emissions have not increased by the same proportion.\nLecturer: How do researchers measure this material? A filter sampler draws a known volume of air through a filter. Laboratories can analyse filter samples for ions such as nitrate and ammonium. The method supplies chemical composition, not merely the total mass of fine particles.\nLecturer: Total particle mass remains valuable, but it cannot by itself identify which chemical components produced a peak. Two days may have similar fine-particle mass while one contains more nitrate and the other more organic material or sulfate. Composition measurements therefore change the scientific interpretation.\nLecturer: Filters also introduce uncertainty. Ammonium nitrate is semi-volatile. Material collected on a filter can partly evaporate during sampling, transport or storage, especially if temperature changes. A low laboratory result may therefore reflect loss from the filter rather than low nitrate in the original air.\nLecturer: The opposite problem can occur when gases are retained by the sampling system and later counted as particles. Researchers use blanks, careful handling and comparison instruments to estimate these artefacts. No single correction works perfectly under all atmospheric conditions.\nLecturer: Continuous instruments provide finer time resolution than a twenty-four-hour filter. They can reveal a morning rise or a rapid response to changing weather. However, those instruments also require calibration and may infer composition indirectly. High time resolution does not automatically mean lower uncertainty.\nLecturer: For that reason, a strong study combines methods. Filter analysis gives detailed chemical evidence; continuous instruments show short-term change; meteorological observations help explain mixing and temperature. Agreement among them is more persuasive than a single dramatic reading.\nLecturer: Researchers also compare sites. A roadside station reflects nearby traffic more strongly, while an urban-background station samples a broader mixture. Yet a higher nitrate value beside a road does not prove that all nitrate was emitted there, because secondary formation can occur during transport.\nLecturer: Policy interpretation must separate precursor control from particle measurement. Reducing nitrogen oxides can alter nitric-acid production, while reducing ammonia changes neutralisation. The response may be nonlinear because other atmospheric compounds compete for ammonia and because weather varies between years.\nLecturer: When comparing winters, scientists therefore avoid attributing every decline to one regulation. They examine emissions, temperature, humidity, inversion frequency and the performance of the instruments. A colder year could increase the particle fraction even if precursor emissions fell.\nLecturer: The central lesson is methodological. Nitrate is produced through linked chemical steps, distributed by moving air and observed through imperfect instruments. The best explanation joins the precursor record, the meteorology and more than one measurement method rather than relying on total particle mass alone. It should also report uncertainty openly, distinguish correlation from mechanism, and test whether the same interpretation survives at different sites and times. That caution matters whenever a chemically dynamic pollutant is represented by a single daily number.",
