      "passage": "A Removing water from fruit juice makes storage and transport more efficient, but the usual method of evaporation exposes flavour compounds to heat. Freeze concentration takes another route. As a juice is cooled, relatively pure water forms ice while sugars, acids, pigments and most aroma compounds remain in the liquid. Separating the ice therefore leaves a smaller volume with a higher proportion of dissolved solids. The principle sounds simple, yet a useful plant must control where crystals begin, how quickly they grow and how much valuable liquid travels out with them. It is a separation process rather than merely an exercise in making juice cold.\n\nB The process begins in a chilled feed tank, where the incoming juice can be mixed into a uniform batch and coarse particles removed. A pump then sends it through a heat exchanger that recovers cold from outgoing streams and lowers the temperature before crystallisation. Engineers monitor dissolved solids because their concentration affects both product quality and the temperature at which freezing begins. Fruit juice does not freeze at one fixed point like pure water. As the liquid becomes more concentrated, the freezing point falls, so later stages require progressively colder conditions. Pre-cooling reduces the load on the main crystalliser and helps avoid abrupt, poorly controlled freezing.\n\nC In one common arrangement, the juice enters a scraped-surface crystalliser. Refrigerant flows through a cooling jacket around the chamber, and a thin frozen layer forms on the cold inner wall. Rotating scraper blades remove that layer before it becomes a solid block. The fragments return to the moving liquid and act as sites for further crystal growth. Gentle cooling and sufficient residence time favour larger crystals, which are easier to separate than a cloud of tiny particles. If cooling is too rapid, numerous small crystals appear; their combined surface can trap more concentrated liquid. Agitation must therefore keep the mixture mobile without breaking the growing crystals excessively.\n\nD The result is an ice slurry: crystals suspended in concentrated juice. It is pumped from the crystalliser into a wash column, where carefully controlled pressure makes the solid and liquid phases move differently. Pressure or mechanical action compacts the crystals into a dense porous bed. Mother liquor drains from spaces between compacted crystals back towards the concentration circuit. This is the critical separation step. A crystal may be almost pure water internally, but concentrate clinging to its surface represents lost product. Merely collecting the visible ice would consequently give a poor yield and produce a waste stream rich in flavour and sugar.\n\nE Near the top of the column, a small quantity of wash water moves against the compacted crystal bed and displaces adhering mother liquor. The cleaned ice crosses a melting zone and leaves as ice melt, while the concentrated juice is collected separately at the concentrate outlet. Some systems recycle part of either stream to stabilise flow. The balance is delicate: too little washing sacrifices product, whereas excessive washing can dilute the concentrate and increase the energy needed for another pass. Operators compare the solids content of the feed, concentrate and melt stream to judge whether the separator is recovering juice effectively.\n\nF A single pass cannot usually remove all the desired water. As concentration rises, viscosity increases and pumping becomes harder; the lower freezing point also demands more refrigeration. Plants may therefore use several stages, each operating at a colder condition, or stop at a moderate concentration that protects quality. Crystal size is affected by residence time, temperature difference and the amount of surface available for growth. These variables interact. Extending residence time can enlarge crystals, for example, but also reduces throughput unless equipment volume increases. Successful design is a compromise among separation efficiency, energy use, product loss and the rate of production.\n\nG The chief attraction is the low processing temperature. Volatile aromas that might be driven away during evaporation can remain with the concentrate, and heat-sensitive colours or nutrients may be better preserved. This does not make freeze concentration superior. Refrigeration and separation equipment cost money, and washed ice can carry away valuable solids. The technique is most persuasive when freshness commands a premium or when recovered cold can be reused. Its performance must be measured across the whole loop, not inferred from the purity of one crystal. Good results come from coordinating pre-cooling, crystal growth, washing, recycling and product specifications.",
