      "title": "Academic Reading — Passage 3: Paying for Outcomes in Public Services",
      "passageTitle": "Paying for Outcomes in Public Services",
      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A Public agencies have bought inputs or activities: a number of training hours, staffed beds or visits delivered. Outcome-based service contracts shift attention towards a change in people's circumstances, such as sustained employment, stable housing or improved health. Payment may depend on evidence that the change occurred. The attraction is clear. Providers gain room to choose methods, while commissioners appear to purchase social value rather than motion. Yet this apparent simplicity conceals a chain of decisions. Someone must define the outcome, establish a baseline, decide when payment is triggered and determine how much of the observed change can reasonably be linked to the service.\n\nB The problem is measurement. An outcome must represent the purpose but be precise enough to verify. A narrow metric, such as obtaining any job, may reward quick placement while ignoring whether employment lasts or suits the participant. A broad aim, such as improved wellbeing, may capture what matters but be difficult to measure. Combining indicators can reduce one distortion and create another by making the formula opaque. The sensible response is not to abandon measurement, but to treat the metric as a model of success rather than success itself. Contracts need both a payment rule and a wider account of quality that remains visible when one number improves.\n\nC Baseline design determines what counts as progress. If providers work with people who begin from different positions, a single threshold may reward easy cases and make complex support financially unattractive. Adjusting payments for prior circumstances can help, but the model then relies on data and contestable assumptions about expected progress. This is one route to selection behaviour, in which organisations prefer participants likely to meet the target, and parking, in which harder cases receive less attention. Such behaviour is not an inevitable moral failure by providers. It can be a response when a payment formula assigns risk without recognising differences in need.\n\nD Attribution is harder. Employment can rise because the economy improves; health may change through treatment received elsewhere; housing stability can depend on rent levels. A contract can verify that an outcome followed a service without proving that the service caused it. Comparison groups, trends and staged evaluation can strengthen inference, but each has limitations in programmes. Commissioners therefore make a judgment about how much causal certainty is proportionate to the payment at stake. The more money attached to a disputed measure, the stronger the pressure for evidence and challenge. Outcome contracting cannot eliminate uncertainty; it decides how institutions will act while uncertainty remains.\n\nE Risk transfer is presented as a virtue because public funds are paid only after results. In reality, risk does not disappear when it moves from commissioner to provider. Large organisations may finance staff while waiting for delayed payments; small charities may be unable to carry the cash-flow burden even when they understand local needs. Providers also charge for taking risk, so an apparently protective arrangement can raise the price. Public guidance consequently warns against transferring risks to a party that cannot manage them. Allocation should follow control: a provider can influence service quality, but it cannot control a regional recession or every decision made by another agency.\n\nF Payment triggers also shape information. When evidence is collected chiefly to release money, staff have a strong reason to document success but less reason to record near misses, displacement or effects that occur after the measurement window. Verification protects against unsupported claims, yet excessive reporting can divert resources from delivery. Good contract management uses audits and data checks while preserving routes for qualitative evidence from service users. It also defines how disagreements will be resolved before a payment is due. Without an appeal process, the commissioner acts as designer, purchaser and final judge. That concentration of roles may weaken trust even when individual decisions are defensible.\n\nG Supporters argue that provider flexibility encourages innovation. This can happen when the outcome is clear and organisations are free to adapt. It is less convincing when activity rules remain in place alongside outcome risk; providers then face both prescriptive control and uncertain payment. Innovation can make comparison difficult if each organisation delivers a different model. The answer is not to standardise every action, because doing so would restore the input contract by another name. Commissioners can instead specify minimum safeguards, require an explanation of the service model and compare patterns across providers. Learning depends on knowing not only whether a target was met, but also what was tried and for whom.\n\nH The strongest case for outcome contracts is therefore conditional. They can align attention with public goals when outcomes are measurable, the provider has meaningful influence, risk is affordable and monitoring examines unintended effects. They are weaker when attribution is remote, data arrive too late or the payment formula discourages work with people who need the most support. Accountability must extend beyond the trigger that releases funds. Elected bodies and service users need to know how priorities were chosen, who carried risk and what happened outside the metric. An outcome contract is not a substitute for public judgment. It is one governance tool whose incentives, evidence and distributional effects require scrutiny.",
